In Java, we can connect our applications to a MySQL database using JDBC (Java Database Connectivity). JDBC is a standard API that allows Java programs to execute SQL queries, retrieve data, and manipulate databases seamlessly.Java Database Connectivity PrerequisitesBefore setting up JDBC with MySQL, ensure the following:MySQL is installed on your system.Java JDK installed on your system.Downloading MySQL ConnectorFollow these steps to download MySQL Connector/J:Search for MySQL Community Downloads.Go to Connector/J.Select Platform Independent.Download the ZIP Archive.Extract the ZIP file.Locate the JAR file:mysql-connector-java-8.0.20.jarSetting Up Database Connectivity with JDBCUsers have to follow the following steps:Step 1: Create a DatabaseLog in to MySQL and create a database (e.g., mydb):
            Java

    CREATE DATABASE mydb;

Step 2: Create a TableCreate a table in the database, for example:
            Java

    CREATE TABLE designation (
    code INT PRIMARY KEY AUTO_INCREMENT,
    title CHAR(35) NOT NULL UNIQUE
);

Step 3: Set Up Project DirectoryCreate a main project folder, name it gfg. Inside gfg, create two directories:src -> To store Java source fileslib -> To store the MySQL connector JARPut the MySQL connector java jar file in the lib folder.Step 4: JDBC Connectivity BasicsTo connect Java to MySQL, you need:1. Driver Class:com.mysql.cj.jdbc.Driver2. Connection URL:jdbc:mysql://localhost:3306/mydbReplace mydb with your database name.Username & Password: Credentials for your MySQL database.Register Driver and Establish Connection:
            Java

    Class.forName("com.mysql.cj.jdbc.Driver");
Connection connection = DriverManager.getConnection(
    "jdbc:mysql://localhost:3306/mydb", "mydbuser", "mydbuser"
);

Step 5: JDBC Code ExampleIn this src code, we will set up the connection and get all the data from the table. we have created the 'check.java' file in the src folder.Example: Java program to set up connection and get all data from table
            Java

    import java.sql.*;
public class Check {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Connection connection = null;
        try {
            // Load MySQL JDBC driver
            Class.forName("com.mysql.cj.jdbc.Driver");
            // Establish connection
            connection = DriverManager.getConnection(
                "jdbc:mysql://localhost:3306/mydb", "mydbuser", "mydbuser"
            );
            // Create statement
            Statement statement = connection.createStatement();
            // Execute query
            ResultSet resultSet = statement.executeQuery("SELECT * FROM designation");
            // Process results
            while (resultSet.next()) {
                int code = resultSet.getInt("code");
                String title = resultSet.getString("title").trim();
                System.out.println("Code: " + code + " | Title: " + title);
            }
            // Close resources
            resultSet.close();
            statement.close();
            connection.close();
        } catch (Exception e) {
            System.out.println(e);
        }
    }
}

 Step 6: Compile and RunCompile:javac -classpath ..\lib\mysql-connector-java-8.0.20.jar;. Check.javaRun:java -classpath ..\lib\mysql-connector-java-8.0.20.jar;. CheckNote: Make sure to manually add some sample data in the designation table before running the program.
